; ======================================================================
;  hw_mem.pi3 - the Raspberry Pi 3's answer to "how much memory is
;               fitted", and the one place this board's backend is
;               STRUCTURALLY different from the Pi 4's.
;
;  The contract is the HwMemory* block in Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi: one total in
;  BYTES, or -1 when the board honestly cannot know, and a fragment
;  saying where the number came from.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  WHERE THE NUMBER COMES FROM, AND WHY NOT FROM THE OBVIOUS PLACE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE MAILBOX HAS A TAG THAT LOOKS LIKE THE ANSWER AND IS NOT. "Get ARM
;  memory" ($00010005) reports the ARM's SHARE after the VideoCore split
;  that gpu_mem in config.txt sets. On a stock 1 GB Pi 3 it answers about
;  948 MB, and a monitor that printed that as the board's memory would be
;  wrong and would sound completely sure of itself. This file does not
;  ask it. RaspberryPi3/Board/memmap.pi3 does, for a different question -
;  where the payload window has to stop - and that is the question that
;  tag really answers.
;
;  THE REVISION CODE IS THE ANSWER. The firmware's board revision word
;  carries a MEMSIZE field in bits 22:20, documented by Raspberry Pi
;  under "Raspberry Pi hardware - New-style revision codes". The field is
;  an exponent: the size is 256 MB shifted left by it, so 0 is 256 MB, 1
;  is 512 MB, 2 is 1 GB, 3 is 2 GB, 4 is 4 GB and 5 is 8 GB. Consulted,
;  not copied. A Raspberry Pi 3 Model B v1.2 is code 2.
;
;  DECODED AS A SHIFT AND NOT AS A TABLE, exactly as
;  RaspberryPi4/Board/hw_mem.pi4 decodes the same field: a table answers
;  nothing for the first board that ships a size nobody here has seen,
;  and two decoders of one field must not be able to disagree. The
;  procedure below is a character-for-character twin of the Pi 4's,
;  because it is the same field in the same word from the same firmware.
;
;  AN OLD-STYLE CODE ANSWERS -1. Bit 23 says which scheme the word is in.
;  An old-style code is a small board number from a table this monitor
;  does not carry, and there is no memory field in it at all.
;
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  THE DIFFERENCE FROM THE Pi 4, AND IT IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The Pi 4's version asks the mailbox LAZILY, on the first call, from
;  inside the address guard, and remembers. It can: its mailbox works
;  whenever it is asked.
;
;  THIS BOARD'S MAILBOX STOPS ANSWERING THE MOMENT THE MMU COMES ON.
;  RaspberryPi3/Lib/mailbox.pbi's Pi3MailboxContext() reads SCTLR, masks
;  it with 5 - the MMU bit and the D-cache bit - and REFUSES the
;  transaction if either is set, because the property buffer has to be
;  memory the VideoCore and the ARM agree about and that file makes no
;  claim to support a cached one. The whole argument, and the boot order
;  it forces, is in the ORDER section of RaspberryPi3/Lib/mmu.pi3.
;
;  SO THIS FILE IS ASKED ONCE, DELIBERATELY, DURING BOOT, BEFORE THE MMU.
;  HwMemAsk() is the call the board's Main() makes at the right moment;
;  HwMemoryBytes() afterwards only reads what was remembered. If nobody
;  asked, it says so rather than trying and failing - a mailbox call that
;  refuses looks identical to a firmware that would not answer, and
;  reporting the second when the first happened would send somebody
;  looking at their config.txt for a bug in this file.
;
;  A LAZY ASK WOULD NOT MERELY FAIL. It would fail from inside
;  HwAddrCheck(), which stands in front of every command that takes an
;  address, and it would fail on every one of them, silently, having
;  already decided the answer is unknown. That is why the seam is split
;  into an ask and a read here and is not on the Pi 4.
;
;  INCLUDE ORDER: after RaspberryPi3/Lib/mailbox.pbi (Pi3MailboxCall,
;  pi3_property, pi3_mailbox_outstanding) and after Anvil/Hal/hal.pbi
;  (#HW_MEM_UNKNOWN), and BEFORE RaspberryPi3/Board/hw_addr.pi3, which
;  reads it. House rule 10: this file includes nothing itself.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  What has been asked, and what came back.
;
;  gPiMemAsked is 0 until HwMemAsk() has run and 1 afterwards, whatever
;  the answer was. gPiMemBytes is meaningless until then and is
;  deliberately NOT initialised to a plausible size: an uninitialised
;  read must not look like an answer. *gPiMemWhy is the fragment
;  HwMemoryWhere() returns and is never 0 once asked.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Global gPiMemAsked.i
Global gPiMemBytes.i
Global *gPiMemWhy
Global gPiMemRev.i

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMemFromRevision(rev) - the decode, and nothing else.
;
;  IT IS A PROCEDURE OF ITS OWN because it is the only part of this that
;  a desk gate can EXECUTE. Everything around it talks to the firmware,
;  and a firmware is a board. This is pure - two shifts and a mask on one
;  word - so tools/pi3_hw_memory_emitted_check.py compiles this exact
;  procedure, runs the emitted A64 over every MEMSIZE code and over an
;  old-style word, and rejects a wrong shift, a wrong base and an
;  off-by-one code. A decode nothing can test is a decode nobody should
;  trust.
;
;  268435456 IS 256 MB WRITTEN OUT. It is a literal rather than a
;  constant because a constant in this compiler takes no arithmetic in
;  its initialiser, so "256 * 1024 * 1024" cannot be written where a
;  reader would want it.
;
;  THE SHIFT IS OF A POSITIVE VALUE: rev is a firmware word below
;  $01000000 by the time the field is taken, so ">>" being arithmetic
;  cannot bite.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMemFromRevision(rev.i)
  Define mem.i
  ; NEW-STYLE CODES ONLY. Bit 23 is the scheme bit; an old-style word has
  ; no memory field at all.
  If (rev & $00800000) = 0
    ProcedureReturn #HW_MEM_UNKNOWN
  EndIf
  mem = (rev >> 20) & 7
  ProcedureReturn 268435456 << mem
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  pi3MemRevision() - the firmware's board revision word, or 0.
;
;  Property tag $00010002, "Get board revision": no request words, one
;  32-bit response word. The reply header is checked the same way every
;  other tag in this tree is - tag, length, and the response bit set with
;  the right byte count - because a firmware that answered a different
;  tag would otherwise have its answer decoded as a memory size.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i pi3MemRevision()
  Define buffer.i
  If pi3_mailbox_outstanding <> 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  buffer = ((@pi3_property[0] + 15) >> 4) << 4
  PokeL(buffer, 28)
  PokeL(buffer + 4, 0)
  PokeL(buffer + 8, $10002)
  PokeL(buffer + 12, 4)
  PokeL(buffer + 16, 0)
  PokeL(buffer + 20, 0)
  PokeL(buffer + 24, 0)
  If Pi3MailboxCall(buffer, 28) = 0
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  If PeekL(buffer + 8) <> $10002 Or PeekL(buffer + 12) <> 4 Or (PeekL(buffer + 16) & $FFFFFFFF) <> $80000004
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn PeekL(buffer + 20) & $FFFFFFFF
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMemAsk() - ask the firmware, once, while it can still be asked.
;
;  CALLED BY THE BOARD'S Main() BEFORE THE MMU COMES ON. It answers 1 if
;  a size is now known and 0 if not; the board does not stop for a 0,
;  because a monitor that will not come up because it could not learn its
;  memory size is worse than one that says it does not know.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMemAsk()
  Define rev.i

  If gPiMemAsked <> 0
    ProcedureReturn Bool(gPiMemBytes <> #HW_MEM_UNKNOWN)
  EndIf
  gPiMemAsked = 1
  gPiMemBytes = #HW_MEM_UNKNOWN

  rev = pi3MemRevision()
  gPiMemRev = rev
  If rev = 0
    *gPiMemWhy = "the firmware would not say what board this is"
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  gPiMemBytes = HwMemFromRevision(rev)
  If gPiMemBytes = #HW_MEM_UNKNOWN
    *gPiMemWhy = "an old-style revision code carries no memory field"
    ProcedureReturn 0
  EndIf

  *gPiMemWhy = "the board revision code the firmware reports"
  ProcedureReturn 1
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMemoryBytes() - the seam. A READ, never a firmware transaction.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMemoryBytes()
  ProcedureReturn gPiMemBytes
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMemoryWhere() - where that number came from, or why there is none.
;
;  IT IS NEVER 0. A caller prints this fragment whenever the size is
;  missing, and a caller that printed nothing would turn "this board
;  cannot say" into a blank space, which reads as a monitor that failed
;  to fill something in rather than as an answer.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMemoryWhere()
  If gPiMemAsked = 0
    ProcedureReturn "the firmware was never asked - this board can only ask before it turns its MMU on, and that moment has passed"
  EndIf
  ProcedureReturn *gPiMemWhy
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMemoryRevision() - the raw word, for `info` and for a bug report.
;
;  Not part of the portable seam; the board's own `info` row prints it
;  because a revision code is the one number that identifies a Pi model,
;  memory size and manufacturer at once, and somebody reporting a wrong
;  size needs to be able to quote it.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwMemoryRevision()
  ProcedureReturn gPiMemRev
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  PutMemBytes(b) - a byte count as a person would say it.
;
;  "1 GB", "512 MB". Not 1073741824, and not 1024 MB: the number on the
;  box is the number to print, and every size this field can carry is an
;  exact power of two multiplying 256 MB, so there is never a remainder
;  to round away or to hide.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure PutMemBytes(b.i)
  Define mb.i
  If b < 0
    Print("an unknown amount")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  mb = b / 1048576
  If mb >= 1024
    PrintDec(mb / 1024)
    Print(" GB")
  Else
    PrintDec(mb)
    Print(" MB")
  EndIf
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwMemSayArm() - the sentence `info` needs that the Pi 4 does not.
;
;  THREE DIFFERENT NUMBERS ARE ALL TRUE AT ONCE ON THIS BOARD and a
;  monitor that blurs them is lying:
;
;    FITTED is what HwMemoryBytes() answers - 1 GB on a Model B v1.2,
;    from the revision code. That is the question "how much memory does
;    this computer have", and it is the RAM row of `info`.
;
;    REACHABLE BY THE ARM is at most $3F000000, because the peripheral
;    aperture sits on the top 16 MB of the gigabyte and - unlike the
;    Pi 4 - that memory does not reappear anywhere. It is gone.
;
;    GIVEN TO THE ARM is less again, by whatever gpu_mem takes for the
;    VideoCore. That is pi3_ram_end, and it is the number the payload
;    window's ceiling comes from.
;
;  The Pi 4 has none of this to explain because its firmware does not
;  carve the ARM's memory the same way, which is why this procedure
;  exists here and has no counterpart there.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwMemSayArm()
  Print("            of which the ARM was given ")
  If pi3_ram_end > 0
    PutMemBytes(pi3_ram_end - pi3_ram_base)
    Print(", ending at ")
    PutAddr(pi3_ram_end - 1)
    PrintNl()
    PrintN("            - the rest is the VideoCore's share, set by gpu_mem, and the")
    PrintN("              top 16 MB of the gigabyte is under the peripheral window and")
    PrintN("              is not reachable by the processor at all.")
    ProcedureReturn
  EndIf
  PrintN("an amount the firmware has not been asked for.")
EndProcedure
